# SurfDoc Diagrams

::summary
The `::diagram` block renders 17 native diagram types and 4 chart aliases as deterministic, dependency-free inline SVG. Bodies written in mermaid syntax are auto-detected and translated to the native DSL. Malformed input never fails a render — it degrades per line (mermaid) or falls back to the preformatted source (native). Layout is pure integer arithmetic: the same document always produces byte-identical output.
::

::callout[type=info title="The determinism promise"]
`::diagram` rendering is a pure function of the block body. No randomness, no timestamps, no hash-map iteration, no text measurement — geometry comes from integer arithmetic over declaration order. Render the same source twice, on any machine, and the SVG bytes are identical. Consumers can (and do) pin exact substrings in tests.
::

## The block

A diagram is a block directive: an opener line `::diagram[type=flowchart title="Publish pipeline"]`, the DSL body, and the `::` closer. A three-line flowchart body looks like this:

```
start [terminator]: Draft
review [diamond]: Approved?
start -> review
```

- `type=` selects the diagram kind (17 native types, 4 chart aliases, or `mermaid`). With no `type`, mermaid bodies are still detected by their header line.
- `title=` becomes the accessible SVG `<title>` and the figure caption.
- **Degradation rule:** an unknown type or a body the parser rejects renders as a preformatted code block of the raw source — a diagram never breaks a page. Mermaid bodies degrade more gently: unsupported lines are skipped one at a time (each skip is surfaced by lint rule `L040`), and only a body with nothing translatable falls back to prose.

---

## Native types

The DSL is line-oriented: blank lines are ignored, every other line is one statement. Ids are `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`; labels are free text.

### architecture

Nodes `id: Label`; edges `a -> b`, `a <-> b`, optional `: Label`. Left-to-right columns by dependency depth.

::diagram[type=architecture title="Request flow"]
web: Web Frontend
api: API Gateway
db: Postgres
web -> api: HTTPS
api -> db: SQL
::

### erd

Entities `name: field1, field2 pk, ...` with `pk`/`fk`/`unique` modifiers; relations `a 1--* b: label` with `1`/`*` cardinalities.

::diagram[type=erd title="Core schema"]
users: id pk, email unique
docs: id pk, owner_id fk
users 1--* docs: owns
::

### flowchart

Nodes `id: Label` or `id [shape]: Label` (shapes: `box`, `diamond`/`decision`, `rounded`/`terminator`); edges `a -> b: label`.

::diagram[type=flowchart title="Review loop"]
draft [terminator]: Draft
check [diamond]: Approved?
ship: Publish
draft -> check
check -> ship: yes
check -> draft: no
::

### sequence

Actors `actor id: Label` (alias `participant`); messages `a -> b: text` (solid) and `a --> b: text` (dashed); lifebars `activate id` / `deactivate id`.

::diagram[type=sequence title="Login"]
actor user: User
actor api: API
user -> api: POST /login
activate api
api --> user: 200 OK
deactivate api
::

### gantt

Optional `section Name` headers; tasks `Label: start, duration` with numeric units or ISO dates (`Label: 2026-01-05, 7`). One convention per body.

::diagram[type=gantt title="Release plan"]
section Build
Backend: 2026-07-10, 8
Frontend: 2026-07-12, 6
section Ship
QA: 2026-07-20, 4
::

### state

Transitions `a -> b: event`; `[*]` is the initial marker as a source and the final marker as a target; optional `id: Label` declarations.

::diagram[type=state title="Order lifecycle"]
[*] -> Cart
Cart -> Paid: pay
Paid -> [*]
::

### mindmap

Indentation is hierarchy: the first line is the root, two spaces (or one tab) per level.

::diagram[type=mindmap title="Surface"]
Product
  Web
    Docs
  Mobile
::

### class

Classes `Name: member, member()` — optional `+`/`-`/`#` visibility, trailing `()` marks methods, a leading bare `enum`/`trait` member sets the stereotype. Relations `A -> B` (association), `A *-> B` (composition), `A o-> B` (aggregation), `A ^-> B` (inheritance).

::diagram[type=class title="Billing"]
Account: +id, +suspend()
PlanTier: enum, Free, Pro
Account -> PlanTier: selects
::

### timeline

One event per line: `marker: Label` (all-integer or all-ISO-date markers) or plain `Label` lines for an ordered, unmarked spine.

::diagram[type=timeline title="Roadmap"]
2026-01: Kickoff
2026-04: Beta
2026-06: Launch
::

### journey

Optional `section Name` lanes; tasks `Label: score` with scores 1–5 drawn as a mood curve.

::diagram[type=journey title="First week"]
section Onboarding
Sign up: 3
Import data: 4
section Daily use
Open app: 5
::

### quadrant

Axis labels `x-axis Low --> High` / `y-axis Low --> High`; cell labels `quadrant-1: Label` … `quadrant-4: Label` (1 = top-right, counter-clockwise); points `Name: x, y` with coordinates in 0–1.

::diagram[type=quadrant title="Effort vs impact"]
x-axis Low effort --> High effort
y-axis Low impact --> High impact
quadrant-2: Quick wins
Dark mode: 0.2, 0.65
Full rewrite: 0.9, 0.3
::

### kanban

Columns `column Name` or flush-left `Name:`; any indented line is a card in the most recent column.

::diagram[type=kanban title="Board"]
column To do
  Write spec
Done:
  Ship v1
::

### usecase

Actors `actor id: Label`, use cases `usecase id: Label`, associations `actor -> case`, and dashed dependencies `case ^-> case: includes` / `: extends`.

::diagram[type=usecase title="Checkout"]
actor customer: Customer
usecase buy: Purchase item
usecase pay: Enter payment
customer -> buy
buy ^-> pay: includes
::

### gitgraph

One statement per line: `commit` / `commit: label`, `branch name` (creates and switches), `checkout name`, `merge name` / `merge name: label`. The current branch starts as `main`; each branch is a horizontal lane and merges connect lanes.

::diagram[type=gitgraph title="Release history"]
commit: init
branch feature
commit: draft
checkout main
merge feature: ship
::

### c4

A styled C4 context/container profile. `person id: Label`, `system id: Label` (append ` [ext]` for external/muted), `container id: Label: tech`, one level of `boundary Name { ... }` grouping (dashed), and edges `a -> b: label`.

::diagram[type=c4 title="Platform context"]
person user: Customer
boundary Platform {
container api: API App: Rust
container db: Database: Postgres
}
system mail: Mail Provider [ext]
user -> api: Uses
api -> db: SQL
api -> mail: SMTP
::

### requirement

SysML-flavoured requirement boxes. `requirement id: Label: text`, `element id: Label`, and mandatory-kind edges `a -> b: satisfies|verifies|refines|traces|contains|derives` drawn dashed with a «kind» label.

::diagram[type=requirement title="Render budget"]
requirement r1: Fast render: SVG in under 5ms
element parser: surf-parse
parser -> r1: satisfies
::

### sankey

Flows `Source -> Target: value` (positive numbers; node names may contain spaces). Columns come from the flow graph; nodes are bars sized by throughput and flows are straight-edged bands, stacked in declaration order.

::diagram[type=sankey title="Energy"]
Wind -> Grid: 40
Solar -> Grid: 30
Grid -> Homes: 50
Grid -> Industry: 20
::

---

## Chart aliases

`type=pie`, `donut`, `radar` and `xychart` accept the same pipe-delimited table body as `::chart` and render through the chart pipeline (`xychart` draws as a line chart):

::diagram[type=pie title="Traffic sources"]
Source | Share
Organic | 52
Referral | 26
Paid | 22
::

---

## Mermaid acceptance

Any `::diagram` body whose first significant line is a mermaid header is translated to the native DSL and rendered natively — no client-side JavaScript, same determinism promise. Use `type=mermaid` to say so explicitly; otherwise the sniff alone decides. Thirteen families are accepted:

::data[format=table]
| Mermaid header | Renders as |
|----------------|------------|
| `flowchart` / `graph` (LR/TD/TB/RL/BT) | flowchart |
| `sequenceDiagram` | sequence |
| `classDiagram` | class |
| `stateDiagram-v2` / `stateDiagram` | state |
| `erDiagram` | erd |
| `gantt` | gantt |
| `mindmap` | mindmap |
| `pie` | pie (chart pipeline) |
| `timeline` | timeline |
| `journey` | journey |
| `kanban` | kanban |
| `quadrantChart` | quadrant |
| `gitGraph` | gitgraph |
::

::diagram[type=mermaid title="Mermaid in, native SVG out"]
flowchart LR
  A[Author writes mermaid] --> B{Header known?}
  B -->|yes| C[Translate to native DSL]
  B -->|no| D[Prose fallback]
  C --> E([Deterministic SVG])
::

Translation covers the common core of each family: node/edge/shape forms with labels for flowcharts, `->>`/`-->>` messages with `participant … as …` and `+`/`-` activation shorthand for sequences, member blocks and the full relation set for class diagrams, `[*]` transitions for state, `||--o{` cardinalities and attribute blocks for ER, dated tasks for gantt, and the straightforward mappings for the rest.

::callout[type=tip title="Per-line degradation"]
Constructs outside that core — subgraphs, notes, `loop`/`alt` frames, styling directives, `after` dependencies, cherry-picks — never fail the diagram. The translator skips the line, renders everything else, and records each skip; `surf-lint` reports them as info-level `L040` diagnostics with the line number and construct name.
::
